# Design Rules

## Grid System

This project uses a shared responsive grid with styled-components:

- **Desktop**: 12 columns
- **Tablet** (≤ 1024px): 8 columns
- **Mobile** (≤ 768px): 4 columns

### Config and components

- **Config**: `src/grid/config.js` exports `GRID` — columns, max-width, padding, gaps, and breakpoints per tier, plus pre-built media-query strings (`GRID.MEDIA_TABLET`, `GRID.MEDIA_MOBILE`).
- **Layout**: Import `Grid`, `GridCell`, and helpers from `src/grid` (barrel: `src/grid/index.js`).
- **Hook**: `useMediaQuery(query)` from `src/grid` — subscribe to a media query in JS, e.g. `const isMobile = useMediaQuery(GRID.MEDIA_MOBILE)`.
- **Overlay**: `GridOverlay` in `src/components/GridOverlay.jsx`, rendered in dev only. Press **G** to show/hide the grid for alignment. It reads the same `GRID` config, so it always matches the layout.

### Using the grid

1. Wrap a section in `<Grid>` (a `styled.div` grid container; use `as="section"` etc. to change the element). Pass `$fullBleed` to remove max-width and horizontal padding.
2. Put content inside `<GridCell>` and position it with transient props (1-based column lines):

   **Desktop**
   - `$start` — column start (default 1)
   - `$span` — columns to span; when omitted the cell fills to the last column
   - `$end` — column end line, exclusive (alternative to `$span`)

   **Tablet** (optional; defaults to desktop values against the 8-column grid)
   - `$startTablet` / `$spanTablet` / `$endTablet`

   Placement is clamped to the active breakpoint's column count, so a desktop placement (e.g. `$start={9} $span={4}`) can never overflow the narrower tablet/mobile grids.

   **Mobile** (optional; defaults to full width `1 / -1`)
   - `$startMobile` / `$spanMobile` / `$endMobile`

   **Rows** (optional)
   - `$rowStart` / `$rowSpan` / `$rowEnd`

   **Special**
   - `$subgrid` — the cell becomes a subgrid; its children align to the parent grid's column tracks.

3. Convenience wrappers for common spans (each defaults to full width on mobile): `GridSpan4`, `GridSpan6`, `GridSpan8`, `GridSpan12`. Any `GridCell` prop can still be passed to override.

### Examples

```jsx
import { Grid, GridCell, GridSpan6, GRID, useMediaQuery } from './grid/index.js'

<Grid>
  <GridCell $start={1} $span={8}>...</GridCell>            {/* desktop cols 1–8 */}
  <GridCell $start={9} $span={4}>...</GridCell>            {/* desktop cols 9–12 */}
  <GridCell $start={1} $end={7}>...</GridCell>             {/* cols 1–6 (end exclusive) */}
  <GridCell $span={6} $spanTablet={8} $spanMobile={4}>...</GridCell>
  <GridCell $span={6} $rowSpan={2}>...</GridCell>          {/* spans 2 rows */}
  <GridCell $span={12} $subgrid>...</GridCell>             {/* subgrid, full width */}
  <GridSpan6>...</GridSpan6>                               {/* half width, full on mobile */}
</Grid>

<Grid $fullBleed>...</Grid>  {/* edge-to-edge, no padding/max-width */}
```

- All layout content should align to this grid.
- Max width, padding, and gaps come from `GRID` in `config.js`; never hardcode these values — the overlay matches the same config.

## Responsive Design

- Use the pre-built media-query strings in styled-components: `@media ${GRID.MEDIA_TABLET} { … }` and `@media ${GRID.MEDIA_MOBILE} { … }`.
- Use `useMediaQuery(GRID.MEDIA_MOBILE)` when a breakpoint is needed in JS logic.
- Prefer flexible units (rem, %, clamp) for typography and spacing.
- Cells default to full width on mobile — only pass mobile props when a cell needs a specific placement.

